//
// PROBE: `time.now` and `time.timestamp`.
//
// **A TIME value can be compared and asked its type, and nothing else.** The
// reference gives `Val::Time` arms in equality and in the four orderings
// (`reference/rust_dynamic/src/eq.rs`, `ord.rs`) and none in `conv` or in
// `numeric_op`, so it cannot be printed, converted or added to. Each of those
// refusals ends a program, so they are unit-tested in
// `crates/bund2-stdlib/src/host.rs` and not here.
//
// What is here is what can be asked without ending the program. Comparisons
// read with the **top of the stack as the left operand**: `1 2 <` asks whether
// 2 is less than 1.
//
// `type.of` does not consume its operand, so each is dropped after it — a
// TIME left on the stack would be in the final dump, and `time.now`'s is a
// different number every run (F155).
//
// 13 is TIME.
1700000000 time.timestamp type.of println drop
time.now type.of println drop

// Equal counts are equal; unequal ones are not.
7 time.timestamp 7 time.timestamp == println
1 time.timestamp 2 time.timestamp == println
1 time.timestamp 2 time.timestamp != println

// Ordered by count.
1 time.timestamp 2 time.timestamp < println
2 time.timestamp 1 time.timestamp < println
1 time.timestamp 2 time.timestamp > println
1 time.timestamp 2 time.timestamp >= println
2 time.timestamp 2 time.timestamp <= println

// A TIME is not the integer it was made from.
1 time.timestamp 1 == println

// The clock moves forward: a later reading is not less than an earlier one.
time.now time.now >= println

// Appending to a list is the one arithmetic form that admits it, because the
// list's arm asks nothing of what it is given.
[ 5 ] 1 time.timestamp + len println drop
